{"name":"Mohair Mauve","hex":"#bfa59e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mohair","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Yarn or fabric made from the hair of the angora goat, often as mixed with cotton or other materials.","The long, fine hair of the Angora goat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mohair"},{"term":"mauve","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rich purple synthetic dye, which faded easily, briefly popular c. 1859‒1873 and now called mauveine.","A pale purple or violet colour, like the colour of the dye after it has faded."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a pale purple colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mauve"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mohair","blocks":[{"text":"Mohair is a fabric or yarn made from the hair of the Angora goat. Both durable and resilient, mohair is lustrous with high sheen, and is often blended to add these qualities to a textile. Mohair takes dye exceptionally well. It feels warm in winter due to excellent insulating properties, while moisture-wicking keeps it cool in summer. It is durable, naturally elastic, flame-resistant and crease-resistant. It is considered a luxury fiber, like cashmere, alpaca, angora, and silk, and is more expensive than most sheep's wool."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Ull_mohair.jpg/330px-Ull_mohair.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mohair","wikidata":"Q179628"},{"title":"Mauve","blocks":[{"text":"Mauve is a pale purple color named after the mallow flower. The first use of the word mauve as a color was in 1796–1798 according to the Oxford English Dictionary, but its use seems to have been rare before 1859. Another name for the color is mallow, with the first recorded use of mallow as a color name in English in 1611."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Wilde_Malve.JPG/330px-Wilde_Malve.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mauve","wikidata":"Q604079"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Colored_Angora_Goat.jpg/500px-Colored_Angora_Goat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Colored_Angora_Goat.jpg/500px-Colored_Angora_Goat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An Angora goat","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AColored_Angora_Goat.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Mohair_sign%2C_Camp_Wood%2C_TX_IMG_1326.JPG/500px-Mohair_sign%2C_Camp_Wood%2C_TX_IMG_1326.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Mohair_sign%2C_Camp_Wood%2C_TX_IMG_1326.JPG/1280px-Mohair_sign%2C_Camp_Wood%2C_TX_IMG_1326.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Mohair_sign%2C_Camp_Wood%2C_TX_IMG_1326.JPG/500px-Mohair_sign%2C_Camp_Wood%2C_TX_IMG_1326.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mohair is vital to the economy of the Texas Hill Country, including the Real County community of Camp Wood.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMohair_sign%2C_Camp_Wood%2C_TX_IMG_1326.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Merrythought_teddy_bear_ironbridges.jpg/500px-Merrythought_teddy_bear_ironbridges.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Merrythought_teddy_bear_ironbridges.jpg/1280px-Merrythought_teddy_bear_ironbridges.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thu